// std/test_fixture.jtr — the OS-facing scaffolding a test needs, and nothing else.
//
// TIER: `std`. Requires a filesystem, an environment, and a shell. This is the
// third file of the `std/test` slice and the second that performs effects:
// `test.jtr` decides (`core`), `test_report.jtr` prints, and this one produces the
// INPUTS a test compares — temp file paths, and the captured output of a command.
//
// WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM `test.jtr`. Because `test.jtr` is `core` and must stay
// linkable with no OS at all. Every function here would break that, and none of
// them is needed to *judge* anything — they only fetch bytes for `test.eq_golden`
// to judge. Keeping the fetch and the judgement in different files is what lets the
// judging half be `@no_alloc`-proven and reusable on a freestanding target.
//
// THE EXPECTED-DIAGNOSTICS RECIPE, which is the reason `capture` exists. There is
// no in-language way to invoke the compiler as a library, so an expected-diagnostic
// test shells out and compares text:
//
//     var pr: process.Process = process.host()
//     let n: usize = test_fixture.temp_path("diag.txt", pbuf)
//     let out: str = from_utf8(pbuf[0 .. n])
//     test_fixture.capture(pr, "jestyrc check bad.jtr", out, cbuf)
//     var text: String = fs.read_text(out)
//     test.eq_golden_all(c, rep, "diagnostics", string_view(text), WANT)
//     string_free(text)
//
// The COMPILER PATH IS THE CALLER'S to supply, deliberately. A test harness knows
// where its compiler is; a library cannot, and guessing (`argv(0)`, a `PATH`
// lookup) would silently test the wrong binary. That is also why this module offers
// no `expect_diagnostics(file, want)` convenience: it would have to invent that
// path, and a helper that runs the wrong compiler is worse than no helper.
//
// TEMP DIRECTORIES ARE NOT HERE, and cannot be yet. There is no `mkdir` intrinsic,
// so a test can name a file inside the OS temp directory (`temp_path`) but cannot
// create a fresh directory to isolate itself in. Creating one via
// `process.run("mkdir …")` would work on both shells and is deliberately NOT done:
// it would make every caller's test depend on shell quoting for a path it did not
// choose. A real `temp_dir()` wants one new intrinsic and the eleven-site recipe in
// docs/stdlib-roadmap.md. Until then, name your temp files distinctly — the suites
// in this repo prefix theirs `jestyr_<module>_<case>`.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/test_fixture_demo.jtr

import "process"
import "fs"
import "env"
import "path"

// The platform's temp directory when the environment names none.
//
// **POSIX does not require `TMPDIR` to be set**, and an unset one does not mean "no
// temp directory" — it means the implementation-defined default, which is `/tmp`
// everywhere in practice and is what C spells `P_tmpdir`. A GitHub Ubuntu runner sets
// none of `TMPDIR`/`TEMP`/`TMP`, which is how this was found.
//
// Windows keeps `"."`: `TEMP` and `TMP` are set by the system for every interactive
// and service account, so reaching the fallback there means something unusual is
// already true and the current directory is the safer guess than a hardcoded path
// that may not exist.
@cfg(posix)  fn temp_default() -> str { return "/tmp" }
@cfg(windows) fn temp_default() -> str { return "." }

// The OS temp directory: `TMPDIR` (POSIX), else `TEMP` or `TMP` (Windows), else the
// platform default. A view into the process environment — OS-owned storage, nothing
// to free, the same contract as `env.get`.
//
// All three spellings are checked because no single one is portable: on this repo's
// Windows box `TMPDIR` is unset while `TEMP` and `TMP` are both set.
//
// **The old fallback was `"."` on both platforms, and that was wrong twice over.** It
// made `temp_path` produce a RELATIVE path, so callers wrote their temp files into
// whatever the current directory happened to be — a working tree, or something
// read-only — instead of into a temp directory. And it was reachable: the CI runner
// sets none of the three, so `test_fixture_demo` asserted `is_abs` of the joined path
// and got 0. The old comment said POSIX has `TMPDIR` set "the other way round" from
// Windows; that is true of a login shell and not of a runner.
pub fn temp_dir() -> str {
    let a: str = env.get("TMPDIR")
    if a.len > 0 { return a }
    let b: str = env.get("TEMP")
    if b.len > 0 { return b }
    let c: str = env.get("TMP")
    if c.len > 0 { return c }
    return temp_default()
}

// `<temp_dir()>/<name>` written into `buf`, returning the byte count — the
// buffers-in idiom, so this module allocates nothing.
//
// The separator written is always `/`, because that is what `std/path.join` emits
// and both Windows and POSIX accept it. REQUIRES
// `buf.len >= temp_dir().len + name.len + 1`.
pub fn temp_path(read name: str, mut buf: []u8) -> usize {
    return path.join(temp_dir(), name, buf)
}

// Run `cmd`, redirecting BOTH its stdout and its stderr into `out_path`, and return
// the raw platform status. `buf` is scratch for building the command line and must
// hold `cmd.len + out_path.len + 11`.
//
// Both streams are captured together (`> file 2>&1`) because a diagnostic test
// wants everything the tool said, and which stream a given message chose is the
// tool's business. `2>&1` and the quoted redirect are spelled identically in
// cmd.exe and POSIX sh.
//
// NO SPACE BEFORE THE `>`, and that is not a style choice. cmd.exe's `echo` treats
// everything after `echo ` literally, INCLUDING a space that precedes the redirect
// operator, so `echo hi > "f"` writes "hi " with a trailing space while
// `echo hi> "f"` writes "hi". POSIX sh tokenizes `>` as an operator either way, so
// omitting the space is portable and captures what the command actually said rather
// than what the shell's quoting added. Found by a demo whose golden compare failed
// on exactly one trailing byte.
//
// Goes through the `Process` capability like any other command, so a `denied()`
// handle captures nothing and writes no file — which is what makes a
// capture-based test safe to hand a denied handle.
pub fn capture(mut p: process.Process, read cmd: str, read out_path: str, mut buf: []u8) -> i32 {
    var n: usize = 0
    for i in 0..cmd.len {
        buf[n] = cmd[i] as u8
        n = n + 1
    }
    let mid: str = "> \""
    for j in 0..mid.len {
        buf[n] = mid[j] as u8
        n = n + 1
    }
    for k in 0..out_path.len {
        buf[n] = out_path[k] as u8
        n = n + 1
    }
    let tail: str = "\" 2>&1"
    for m in 0..tail.len {
        buf[n] = tail[m] as u8
        n = n + 1
    }
    return process.run(p, from_utf8(buf[0 .. n]))
}
